{
  "image_name": "03eca6c60b4cfef08c9135afdf2c1dbe.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes a vagovagal reflex circuit linking the gut to the brainstem and back to the stomach. Sensory, or afferent, vagus fibers arising from the nodose ganglion carry signals from the stomach into the medulla, where they terminate in the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS), near the area postrema and tractus solitarius. Within this brainstem region, the incoming signal appears to be processed by several neurotransmitter systems, including glutamate, GABA, and noradrenaline, which influence communication between the NTS and the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus (DMV).\n\nMotor, or efferent, vagus fibers then project from the DMV back to the stomach. The stomach is shown receiving two main vagal outputs: a cholinergic pathway releasing acetylcholine (ACh), associated with excitatory control, and a non-adrenergic, non-cholinergic (NANC) pathway, associated with inhibitory control. A dashed ACh-related element near the stomach suggests modulation of gastric function through local cholinergic mechanisms as well.\n\nOverall, the image conveys how gastric sensory information is integrated in the brainstem and transformed into coordinated autonomic output that regulates stomach activity.",
